Hello all,

I've seen many complain that an intelligent pet behaves no better than a cat or
a dog. So why not have a general mechanism of interacting with monsters as
follows: 

1. A 'give' command which would offer an item (armor, weapon, potion etc). 
   If the monster is hostile, it would take it and may use it to attack you.
   There could be a chance of making the monster peaceful or tame depending on
   the item.

2. A 'take' command which would ask the monster to give an item in its
   possession. This would not work at all with hostile monsters. It would
   always work with tame monsters, but with a possibility of angering them
   (depending on monster level and the item being asked back). It would always
   anger peaceful monsters.

3. The monster would always try to use any item that is given away (with the
   usual restrictions of 2/? rings, 1 amulet, 1/? weapon etc etc).

4. A monster would not drop any item its wearing. But would drop any other item
   as usual. IMHO, this would let pets not to  be used as pack mules.

5. A 'look at' command (I believe this was mentioned by some irate hackers who
   got 'But wait!! the ***'s medallion begins to glow ..' message) which would
   tell what items the monster carries. For tame monsters, this would list all
   the items in its inventory. For others, only the visible items.

6. If a piece of armor or a weapon is given while having an equivalent item,
   the monster would use the better one. After all, they know wands, potions
   and scrolls now. Why not armor and weapons.

7. A tame monster would use an item only if it was given to it. This would
   avoid grabbing good armor and weapons before the player. 

So, what do you think? (I know, the hard part is convincing someone to
implement these fancy stuff). 

IMHO, these changes would not affect the game balance as it is now. Further,
the code is already there in self polymorphing parts and item using monster
parts. Its just a matter of applying it in a different context (at least thats
my uneducated opinion).

